@article{hartley2023policy,
    title = {Policy is theft: The state of global Internet policy in an age of revolutions},
    author = {Hartley, John},
    year = {2023},
    journal = {Policy \& Internet},
    volume = {15},
    number = {4},
    pages = {591--610},
    issn = {1944-2866},
    doi = {10.1002/poi3.364},
    url = {https://doi.org/10.1002/poi3.364},
    language = {en},
    keywords = {internet governance, Fourth Industrial Revolution, datafication, platformization, metaverse, social question, Revolutions of 1848, constitutionalism, consumer rights, digital inclusion},
    abstract = {Men must learn now with pity to dispense; For policy sits above conscience. (Shakespeare, Timon of Athens, III.ii.91-3). tech corporations and platforms, which see the global internet as a private market; state legislatures and regulatory systems, which advance national and sectional interests; consumer rights, where ‘consumer’ means individual shoppers, not creative citizens or classes acting at global scale. There is a limit to what piecemeal reform of existing arrangements can achieve. That limit is reached when global challenges confront both the living planet (the biosphere) and humanity (as a species). The current horizons of policy are set by administrative, not constitutional parameters. As important as the reality and quantity of suffering were the ways in which this era saw and tabulated social dysfunction. The ‘Social Question’ that preoccupied mid-nineteenth-century Europeans was a constellation of real-world problems, but it was also a way of seeing. (Clark, 2023, p. 15) ‘Ways of seeing’ are not simply empirical descriptions of existing realities, they are also ways of imagining alternative possibilities, including new ways of constituting a polity or the relations among them, and ‘ways of seeing’ are notoriously cultural in origin (Berger, 1972). This constitutional struggle is the ‘Social Question’ of emergent industrial modernity (Clark, 2019), which reached its emblematic climax in the ‘year of revolutions’, 1848. The First Industrial Revolution used water and steam power to mechanize production. The Second used electric power to create mass production. The Third used electronics and information technology to automate production. Now a Fourth Industrial Revolution is building on the Third, the digital revolution that has been occurring since the middle of the last century. It is characterized by a fusion of technologies that is blurring the lines between the physical, digital, and biological spheres. (Schwab, 2016) From this perspective, technology has enabled the exploitation of energy resources (steam, oil, electricity and electronics) to mechanise, scale up, automate, and globalise industrial production, transforming the production of value from materials to information. In the process, not only the social and cultural, but also the ‘physical, digital, and biological spheres’ are now under the sway of technology, which stages its own revolutions, each more abstract—but more pervasive and unarguable—than the one before. It seems nothing can escape the exorbitant logic of ‘datafication, platformization, metaverse’, but meanwhile, much of humanity seems to be missing. Agency is produced and reproduced by automation, AI, and robots, resulting in what Harari (2017) famously dubbed ‘the rise of the useless class’. Does this mean that those he calls the ‘unworking class’ are no longer human; discounted along with a succession of pitiful others who've been disenfranchised and dispossessed by imperial-colonial industrialisation and its successors, including traditional cultures and First Nations, women and girls, migrants and refugees, and what they used to call ‘the poor’, in the guise of welfare dependants and ‘the Global South? In line with presumptions of the Fourth Industrial Revolution, the internet is construed as a technological invention. That means its infrastructure, platforms, and applications can be owned—unlike language, culture, and sociality. Thus, ‘global internet policy’ (insofar as it exists, through ICANN, for instance) is administered through the ‘way of seeing’ of intellectual property, a lens that cannot observe the biosphere, humanity, or global crises as part of its ‘sphere’ of action. Thus, reducing the internet to property means that policy is part of the apparatus of appropriation, accumulation, and enclosure, or privatisation, of language, culture and mediation in the name of sectional interests, favoured nations, and powerful corporations. In short, extending the classic critique of Pierre-Joseph Proudhon, it can be argued that policy is theft. In the emergent Anthropocene era, an alternative way of seeing is needed to address the global impact of challenges facing the climate in the geosphere, extinctions in the biosphere, and human rights in the semiosphere. In this article, I return to the ‘social question’ of ‘global internet policy’ at a constitutional level. The ‘modern’ alternative to property capitalism has been revolution from the get-go. What are its prospects now? Those who agitate for system change—notably activists for climate justice, environmental and human rights—have been dismissed as part of the useless class (not least by one of the reviewers of this article), because their proclaimed goal has not been achieved, either politically or industrially. But is that ‘end of story’, as the realists would have it? Or is there another way to constitute ‘global internet policy’? Here, a lot depends on how you imagine the future. Modernist revolutionary filmmaker Jean-Luc Godard was once asked, ‘But surely you agree, M. Godard, that films should have a beginning, a middle part and an end?’ He answered: ‘Yes, but not necessarily in that order’.1 Here, I follow that Godardian rubric. As Collins put it (2010, p. 687): ‘In some ways it is as if Hartley is visiting from the future and sharing his observations’. Here, I do not proceed from past, through present to future. Instead, I begin with the future and end with the past, to remediate the present. It may be the case that any real “metaverse” would be little more than some cool VR games and digital avatars in Zoom calls, but mostly just something we still think of as the internet. (Wired, April 2022) The tech industry writ large depends on futurism. Selling a phone is fine, but selling the future is more profitable. (Wired, April 2022) To find out ‘whether this next generation of the internet will ever really materialize’, Wired interviews Matthew Ball, a former Amazon exec, ‘strategist, venture capitalist, and author’, whose book, The Metaverse And How It Will Revolutionize Everything, was published in 2022.2 Revolutionising used to be something that societies did to governments. Now, it's something the tech giants hope to do to us. Revolutionaries and big-tech corporations do share one key characteristic: their investment in the future. Both hope to hasten system change; and both, as early adopters, hope that they'll be the ones to pull the levers that operate the new ‘everything’. What is often dismissed as hype, in fact, is just another word for the monetisation of speculative futures. It is so predicable that it has been copyrighted, as the Gartner (2022) Hype Cycle (Figure 1). According to the 2022 edition, the metaverse is still more than a decade away, so there's plenty of future to sell. It follows that for something not to exist in real life, but only in the way it is represented, is no impediment to investment. Indeed, it's the very essence of copyright. Thus, ‘revolutionising everything’ is to be an effect, not of collective action for the emancipation of civil society, but of corporate action for the extension of intellectual property (Johns, 2010; Wheeler, 2019). Comrades, the revolution will be privatised. At the same time, experience shows that ‘selling the future’ is quite risky, as Wall Street discovered in March 2000, when Silicon Valley failed to ‘revolutionise everything’ quickly enough to prevent the dot-com crash. Even so, the dot-com bubble was not a failure of the internet. It was an artefact of the US stock market. Relentless marketing and copycat entrepreneurship (a.k.a. ‘Silicon Valley’) had overvalued start-up companies sufficiently for venture-capitalists to cash out their profit by selling their shares to retail investors. These so-called ‘mums and dads’ were the ones who bought into the risk and lost the money that sent the NASDAQ into a nosedive from which it took 15 years to recover. During that time, the internet settled down as a corporatised ‘internet of shopping’, as share prices slowly recovered. Hype proves to be the very medium through which disruptive experimentation can proliferate in a period of system change. According to Gartner (Figure 1), after the curve of ‘inflated expectation’ (the venture-capital stage), there's a ‘trough of disillusionment’ (the retail-loser stage), followed by a ‘slope of enlightenment’ (learning how to monetise apps) and finally a ‘plateau of productivity’ (corporate profitability). In Gartner's version of the future, the government plays no part. There's no such thing as ‘public’ administration. The needle that pricked the bubble at the ‘peak of inflated expectation’ was not state regulation, but what Gartner calls ‘negative press’. The ‘hype cycle’ model is governed by market forces. One of them is media coverage (a.k.a. ‘market information’), when investors ‘buy the rumour, sell the news’. While public authorities are treated as superfluous, relegated to the ‘unworking class’, private governance has become a rapidly digitising process, under the control of CEOs and COOs, not ministers or regulators. In May 2022, Gartner announced that ‘The window is open right now for sewing software and analytics into the fabric of risk governance’.3 Visionaries, foresight consultants, corporate strategists, policy analysts, cultural critics, planners, marketers, forecasters, prediction market developers, roadmappers, operations researchers, investment managers, actuaries, and other risk analyzers, and future-oriented individuals educated in every academic discipline, including anthropology, complexity studies, computer science, economics, engineering, urban design, evolutionary biology, history, management, mathematics, philosophy, physical sciences, political science, psychology, sociology, systems theory, technology studies, trend analysis, and other disciplines (Wikipedia: Futurist)7 This kind of futurism is massively invested in both technical platforms and datafied content (think Cambridge Analytica). The tech giants hire finance futurists to predict probable sources of profit and to manage uncertainty and risk for global operations. ‘Policy’ has migrated from the public sphere to the private, where it can go global as part of corporate strategy. If there is such a thing as ‘global internet policy’, it is being created in their image. Wired calls it ‘the future of talking about the future’. “We still contend with many severe problems, dis- and misinformation, toxicity, harassment, radicalization, happiness at large, data rights, data security, data literacy, platform power, and regulation, the role of algorithms in our lives and how they shape our behaviors. These are all really important problems. And frankly, platform power and platform regulation, we don't have good answers for those either. The metaverse means that they will get worse, harder, at least. (Ball, 2022). KPMG (Ghosh, 2020): “Using tools many organizations are already deploying as part of their digital transformation initiatives—blockchain, cloud, data analytics, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity software, the Internet of Things, and more—organizations can … create a digital trust infrastructure that injects security, auditability, immutability, and rigorous governance into the processes and transactions that flow through their value chains.” Deloitte: “We define digital trust as the confidence among customers, employees, partners, and other stakeholders in an organization's ability to create and maintain the integrity of all digital assets … across stakeholder experiences, strategic insights, organizational platforms, and network connectivity. This digital trust ensures transparency and accessibility, security and reliability, privacy and control, and ethics and responsibility.” PwC: “Digital businesses that lead in safety, security, reliability, privacy and data ethics will be the titans of tomorrow. It is a journey worth taking.” EY: “Without trust, you can't create value. In the Transformative Age, with more data changing hands and more technology used in decision-making, trust is more important than ever. It's trust that enables organizations to create value and capital markets to function properly. With richer insights from deeper data analysis, you can look at risk afresh. You can from what you should to what you can With trust the confidence to strategic It's trust that will you the of is where ‘internet policy’ but not as an of public administration. Instead, trust policy is a digital for risk which in is a of corporate The is the future into and datafied corporate where our may a next generation of the internet … is on … and a (Wired, and It's for reality to that the metaverse is not just for it will also physical of real and early in was from the the as the and it in a where is not The of confidence in the and a of the its a and will do little to the of a some may now be in April While the is still years from the of in the technology and digital at future can get a of in through a it is building in the The will be reality and In effect, will be to into the metaverse and experience as they would in the physical metaverse should more of a he “We don't that the future is where the individual lost … It should be on individual … “We are in he “We have to human into action. “We more than of to be technologies that will power which is what metaverse all In 2022, a new urban for was announced by the This is a that will be and but only on both with to the with and to The will the challenges facing humanity in urban and will a on alternative ways to trust, there is no there is no … technology enables to and the the of their for the of their he 2022) will also be from their and a of other a data that … would information to the and it predict at the of human of at in … data infrastructure not to important social and cultural that are to urban With the also to its from to by of those wil